e  of these cells of the emerging hair  follicle have epifluorescence that is  high and the only cells that have it  that are higher are those  cells that came from the Bulge in the  hair germs so basically that  demonstrates again using another  principle that these stem cells in vivo  without injury or the like just in the  normal hair cycle are contributing to  hair tissue regeneration  we've also then utilized this technology  and coupled it with a series of  staggered nucleotide pulse chases to  basically reconstruct a movie of once  stem cells are activated in exit their  niche where do they go and how do they  contribute to tissue and when along that  lineage do they stop becoming stem cells  and become a committed cell and again  they hair follicles since everything is  spatially and linearly temporally  defined allows us to be able to answer  those questions so it's effectively what  we're doing by doing these staggered  pulse chase experiments is to create  snapshots of the stem cells and the stem  cells as they become these transit  amplifying population of cells what  that's told us is actually that the  whole process of hair regeneration is  one that is driven by these mesenchymal  epithelial stimuli that basically  determine a gradient of proliferation  from the top down and the stem cells get  activated but it's only briefly that  they get activated they go back to  dormancy as the mesenchymal stimulus  moves away the cells that begin moving  further down along the hair follicle get  activated but they chew withdraw from  the cell cycle as the mesenchymal  stimulus goes away and only those cells  now down at the base continue to  proliferate and they're proliferating in  a gradient of rates of proliferation  we've also been able to define when the  hair follicle stem cells stop becoming a  stem cell in this line and that's given  us some very interesting results in  addition we've monitored what happens  when there's a line of 85 cells between  the stem cell niche and the committed  progeny  all of a sudden the destructive phase  sets in and the transit amplifying pool  undergoes a pet OSIS there's a lot of  cells along here and many of them have  divided only a few times they're still  maintaining their stemness properties  and we've shown that they home back to  the stem cell niche we've also shown  however that there are some cells that  have gone too far they still have some  stem cell characteristics but they are  not stem cells they're never going to be  stem cells again and they too home back  to their niche so what's going on  we've learned that basically the cells  in red are the stem cells the cells in  green our cells that had gone too far  along the lineage when the destructive  phase set in and somehow they were  spared from ap ptosis and they go back  to this niche why would a tissue ever do  things this way now the hair follicle is  weird I admit but basically this is  really weird and I think it turns out to  be applicable generally to different  types of stem cell biology so the first  one is not so applicable the first  reason why this inner layer of cells  goes back to the niche and basically is  never going to be stem cells again is  that they have their anchoring that old  hair in place when you think about your  hair growing your hair is growing out  from the surface of your skin if that  process just continues when the  destructive phase sets in and all of  your hair follicles are synchronized  you're gonna undergo cyclic abouts of  baldness with hair growth and baldness  and you think about an animal in the  wild like a grizzly bear and that's not  a good thing for those animals and so  you've got to have a way of anchoring  the hairs in place and what happens is  that by virtue of still maintaining some  of the stem cell characteristics these  cells we think recognize when they get  back to the Bulge to stop and they are  short circuited in their differentiation  and begin to produce the